  <page>
    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Find <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> by implicit differentiation, when
                <md>
                    y\cos(x) = 2x^2 + 4y^2.
                </md>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Differentiating both sides with respect to <m>x</m>, we have
                <md>
                    \frac{d}{dx}(y\cos(x)) = \frac{d}{dx}(2x^2 + 4y^2).
                </md>
                The left-hand side of the above equation is (using the product rule)
                <md>
                    <mrow> \frac{d}{dx}(y\cos(x)) \amp = y(-\sin(x))+ \frac{dy}{dx}\cos(x) </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp = \frac{dy}{dx}\cos(x) - y \sin(x). </mrow>
                </md>
                Differentiating the right-hand side we get
                <md>
                    \frac{d}{dx}(2x^2 + 4y^2) = 4x + 4y \frac{dy}{dx}.
                </md>
                Setting the left-hand side equal to right-hand side, we have
                <md>
                    \frac{dy}{dx}\cos(x) - y \sin(x) = 4x + 4y \frac{dy}{dx}.
                </md>
                Solving for <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m> we get
                <md>
                    \frac{dy}{dx} = \frac{4x + y\sin(x)}{\cos(x)-4y}.
                </md>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The length of a rectangle is increasing at a rate of 8 cm/s and its width is increasing at a rate of 3 cm/s. When the length is 20 cm and the width is 10 cm, how fast is the area of the rectangle increasing?
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                Denote the area by <m>A</m>, the length by <m>l</m> and the width by <m>w</m>. Then
                <md>
                    A = wl \text{ cm}^2.
                </md>
                Differentiating <m>A</m> with respect to <m>t</m> we get
                <md>
                    \frac{dA}{dt} = w \frac{dl}{dt} + \frac{dw}{dt}l.
                </md>
                We are told
                <md>
                    \frac{dl}{dt} = 8 \text{ and } \frac{dw}{dt} = 3.
                </md>
                Hence when <m>l = 20</m> and <m>w = 10</m>, the rate of change of the area is
                <md>
                    \frac{dA}{dt} = (10)(8) + 3(20) = 80 + 60= 140 \text{ cm}^2/\text{sec}.
                </md>
                
            
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    <exercise workspace="0.5in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Suppose <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> is the inverse of a differentiable function <m>f(x)</m>.
                Suppose <m>f(4)= 5</m>, and <m>f'(4)=\frac{2}{3}.</m>
                Find <m>(f^{-1})'(5).</m>
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                The formula for the derivative of the inverse at a point <m>a</m> is
                <md>
                    (f^{-1})'(a) = \frac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(a))}.
                </md>
                As <m>f(4) = 5</m>, <m>f^{-1}(5) = 4</m>.
                Thus
                <md>
                    (f^{-1})'(5) = \frac{1}{f'(f^{-1}(5))} = \frac{1}{f'(4)} = \frac{1}{\frac{2}{3}}=\frac{3}{2}.
                </md>
                
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

    </page>
    <page>

    <exercise workspace="2in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                The radius of a sphere is measured to be <m>10</m>cm with a possible error in measurement at most <m>0.01</m>cm. 
                Find the maximum error in using this value of the radius to compute the volume of the sphere?
                Recall that the volume <m>V</m> of a sphere with radius <m>r</m> is <m>V = \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3</m>.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                The differential <m>dV</m> is defined by the formula
                <md>
                    dV = \left(\frac{dV}{dr}\right) dr.
                </md>
                If <m>V = \frac{4}{3}\pi r^3</m>, then
                <md>
                    \frac{dV}{dr} = 4\pi r^2.
                </md>
                With <m>r = 10</m> and <m>dr = 0.01</m>, we get
                <md>
                <mrow> dV \amp = 4\pi r^2 dr </mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = 4\pi (10)^2 (0.01)</mrow>
                <mrow> \amp = 4\pi.</mrow>
                </md>
                Thus the maximum error in calculating the volume is <m>4 \pi</m> cm<m>^3</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
    </exercise>


        <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>f(x) = \sqrt{1+2x}</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task  workspace="1.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the linear approximation <m>L(x)</m> of <m>f</m> at <m>a=4</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The function <m>L(x)</m> is the tangent line of the curve <m>y = f(x)</m> at the point <m>(4,f(4))</m>.
                    Note
                    <md>
                        f(4) = \sqrt{1 + 8} = 3,
                    </md>
                    <md>
                        f'(x) = \frac{1}{2\sqrt{1+2x}}2 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{1+2x}},
                    </md>
                    and 
                    <md>
                        f'(4) = \frac{1}{3}.
                    </md>
                    Hence
                    <md>
                        L(x) = f'(4)(x-4) + f(4) = \frac{1}{3}(x-4) + 3.
                    </md>
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task  workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Approximate <m>f(4.1)</m> (using your linear approximation above).
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    <m>f(4.1)\approx L(4.1)</m>.
                    Hence
                    <md>
                        f(4.1) \approx \frac{1}{3}(4.1 - 4) + 3 = \frac{1}{30} + 3 = \frac{91}{30}.
                    </md>
                    
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </exercise>


  </page>
